# Line endings are normalised in the repository so a checkout on macOS or
# Windows produces the same files. Windows batch scripts must keep CRLF.
#
# The catch-all `* text=auto eol=lf` is what actually governs the repository:
# git decides text/binary by content, and every text file is stored with LF in
# the index and checked out with LF. The explicit per-extension rules below do
# not change that result -- they are here so the intent survives a future edit
# of the catch-all, and so `git check-attr text eol -- <file>` answers from the
# file's own rule rather than from an inherited one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Source, data and metadata: LF everywhere, on every platform.
*.py   text eol=lf
*.pyi  text eol=lf
*.go   text eol=lf
*.mod  text eol=lf
*.sum  text eol=lf
*.spec text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ini  text eol=lf
*.cfg  text eol=lf
*.fa   text eol=lf
*.fq   text eol=lf

# Windows scripts: cmd.exe is not reliable with LF-only line endings, so these
# are checked out as CRLF. They are still stored as LF in the index.
# NOTE: `eol=crlf` is applied on *checkout* only. A worktree copy that an editor
# wrote directly keeps whatever the editor wrote, so the file on disk can be LF
# while the attribute says CRLF. Verify with:
#     git ls-files --eol -- run.bat        # expect: i/lf w/crlf attr/text eol=crlf
# If it reports `w/lf`, the checkout has to be redone (delete the file and
# `git checkout -- run.bat`) or the editor has to save it with CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Never run text conversion, diff or merge logic over binaries.
# `binary` is git's macro for `-text -diff -merge`.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.gz   binary
*.bz2  binary
*.xz   binary
*.zst  binary
*.zip  binary
*.7z   binary
*.pdf  binary

# The command/parameter schema is generated by tools/spec-dumper (Go) and
# tools/extract_spec.py, so it is not hand-edited and should not dominate review.
#
# What this attribute does, precisely (GitHub, not git, implements it):
#   * linguist-generated -> the file's diff is collapsed by default on the PR /
#     commit page, and the file is excluded from the repository's language
#     statistics. Without it this ~370 KB JSON outweighs the ~225 KB of Python
#     and the About panel would report the project as JSON.
#   * `linguist-vendored` / vendor is deliberately NOT set. Both flags remove a
#     file from language statistics, so adding vendored here would be redundant,
#     and vendored carries the separate meaning "third-party code that is not
#     developed in this repository" -- commands.json is generated from upstream
#     source but is a build product of *this* repository, and marking it vendored
#     would misdescribe what maintainers are expected to regenerate.
#   * git itself ignores `linguist-*`: they are metadata that GitHub reads.
seqkit_gui/data/commands.json linguist-generated=true
